---
// apps/site/src/pages/how-it-works.astro
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How It Works"
  description="How Verdecito works — spec, instances, skins, and render policy."
>
  <div class="page-hero" style="margin-top:2rem">
    <div class="eyebrow">Explainer</div>
    <h1>How It Works</h1>
    <p>Verdecito is a spec plus a reference instance. Here is how the parts connect.</p>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>The spec</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>spec</strong> (<code>spec/</code>) defines object types — seed, quest,
      sprint, task, artifact, ask, channel — and the conventions for naming, frontmatter,
      and lifecycle. It is schema-versioned so instances and renderers can declare
      compatibility.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Instances</h2>
    <p>
      An <strong>instance</strong> is a collection of canonical objects living in
      <code>instances/&lt;name&gt;/</code>. Each object is a Markdown file with YAML
      frontmatter. The Verdecito reference instance at <code>instances/verdecito/</code>
      is the first public example — maintained by the project, curated for public safety.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can fork the repo and run your own instance alongside the spec. That is the
      intended use.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Skins</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>skin</strong> (<code>skins/&lt;name&gt;/</code>) controls how an instance
      is rendered for a particular surface: which objects appear, in what order, with what
      vocabulary. The default skin is <code>skins/verdecito/</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Skins include a <code>render-policy.yaml</code> (what to show), a
      <code>vocabulary.yaml</code> (label overrides — e.g. <em>channel</em> becomes
      <em>door</em>), and a <code>manifest.yaml</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Render policy</h2>
    <p>The current skin's render policy enforces three rules at build time:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Private objects</strong> are excluded. They never reach the public surface.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Teaser objects</strong> expose only title and summary. The body is withheld.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public objects</strong> are rendered in full, including body content.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If a public or teaser object is missing required fields (<code>id</code>,
      <code>type</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>schema_version</code>,
      <code>visibility</code>, <code>lifecycle</code>), the build fails. This prevents
      silent data corruption from reaching the public surface.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only objects with lifecycle <code>active</code> are included. Draft and archived
      objects are excluded.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>This site</h2>
    <p>
      This site is a static <a href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astro</a>
      app at <code>apps/site/</code>. It reads the canonical data from the repo at build
      time, applies the render policy, and outputs static HTML. No database, no server,
      no runtime data fetching.
    </p>
    <p>
      The data layer lives in <code>src/lib/verdecito.ts</code>. Every page calls it —
      the policy is enforced once, uniformly, at build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Fork it</h2>
    <p>
      The entire thing — spec, instance, skin, and site — is on GitHub.
      Fork it, adapt it, run your own instance.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/verdecito-life" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        github.com/verdecito-life →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
